+++
title: Koine Greek
description: Dialect of Greek in the ancient world
hook: The same Greek carried the Septuagint, the Christian New Testament and
  the private thoughts of a Roman emperor. It is still the language of
  services in the Greek Orthodox Church.
standfirst: The common Greek that spread with Alexander's conquests in the
  fourth century BC, and served as the lingua franca of much of the
  Mediterranean region and the Middle East for centuries afterwards. It was
  built mainly on Attic and Ionic speech, with admixtures from contact with
  other varieties.
key-facts:
  - name: Also called
    value: Hellenistic Greek, Biblical Greek, Septuagint Greek or New Testament
      Greek
  - name: In use
    value: c. 300 BC – AD 600 (developed into Medieval Greek, survives as the
      liturgical language of the Greek Orthodox and the Greek Catholic
      churches)
  - name: Spoken across
    value: Hellenistic Greece, Macedon, Ptolemaic Kingdom, Seleucid Empire and
      other Hellenistic Kingdoms, Roman Empire, Eastern Roman Empire
  - name: Spoken by
    value: Greeks
  - name: Written in
    value: Greek alphabet
  - name: ISO 639-3 code
    value: ecg (a proposal to use ecg was rejected in 2023)
pull-quotes:
  - text: ἡ ἐκ τῶν τεττάρων συνεστῶσα
    attribution: Hellenistic scholars, on Koine as the composition of the four
      main Ancient Greek dialects
    section: origins-and-history
  - text: He maintains that ἐκκλησία is merely used for designating the notion of
      meeting and gathering of men, without any particular character.
      Therefore, etymologizing this word could be needless, or even
      misleading, when it could guide to false meanings, for example that
      ἐκκλησία is a name used for the people of God, Israel.
    attribution: Kyriakoula Papademetriou, on James Barr
    section: septuagint-greek
timeline:
  - when: 4th century BC
    what: The start of Koine Greek.
    from: "start-time: 4th century BC"
  - when: 323 BC
    what: The death of Alexander the Great, the conventional beginning of the
      post-Classical period of Greek.
    from: Though elements of Koine Greek took shape in Classical Greece, the
      post-Classical period of Greek is defined as beginning with the death of
      Alexander the Great in 323 BC, when cultures under Greek sway in turn
      began to influence the language.
  - when: 3rd century BC
    what: The Septuagint, the Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible, is made.
    from: Koine is also the language of the Septuagint (the 3rd century BC Greek
      translation of the Hebrew Bible), the Christian New Testament, and of
      most early Christian theological writing by the Church Fathers.
  - when: second century BC
    what: The Ancient Greek pitch accent is replaced with a stress accent, and all
      vowels become isochronic.
    from: The ancient distinction between long and short vowels was gradually
      lost, and from the second century BC all vowels were isochronic (having
      equal length). From the second century BC, the Ancient Greek pitch
      accent was replaced with a stress accent.
  - when: 170 BC
    what: A decree of the Roman Senate to the town of Thisbae in Boeotia, the
      article's sample of conservative mainland Koiné.
    from: The following excerpt, from a decree of the Roman Senate to the town of
      Thisbae in Boeotia in 170 BC, is rendered in a reconstructed
      pronunciation representing a hypothetical conservative variety of
      mainland Greek Koiné in the early Roman period.
  - when: 1st century BC
    what: Koine Greek becomes a language of literature, and some people begin to
      distinguish a literary form from the vernacular.
    from: "When Koine Greek became a language of literature by the 1st century BC,
      some people distinguished two forms: written as the literary
      post-classical form (which should not be confused with Atticism) and the
      vernacular."
  - when: between 175 and 225
    what: The 'most probable date' of Papyrus 46, one of the oldest extant New
      Testament manuscripts in Greek.
    from: Papyrus 46 is one of the oldest extant New Testament manuscripts in
      Greek, written on papyrus, with its 'most probable date' between 175 and
      225.
  - when: 330 AD
    what: Constantine the Great founds Constantinople; the passage into Medieval
      Greek is sometimes dated from here, but often only from the end of late
      antiquity.
    from: The passage into the next period, known as Medieval Greek, is sometimes
      dated from the foundation of Constantinople by Constantine the Great in
      330 AD, but often only from the end of late antiquity.
  - when: After the 4th century
    what: Christianity becomes the state church of the Roman Empire, and more
      learned registers of Koiné come into use.
    from: Christian writers in the earliest time tended to use a simple register
      of Koiné, relatively close to the spoken language of their time,
      following the model of the Bible. After the 4th century, when
      Christianity became the state church of the Roman Empire, more learned
      registers of Koiné also came to be used.
  - when: 6th century
    what: The end of Koine Greek.
    from: "end-time: 6th century"
  - when: 10th century AD
    what: υ and οι unround to merge with ι, completing the changes known as
      iotacism.
    from: In the 10th century AD, υ/οι/y/ unrounded to merge with ι. These changes
      are known as iotacism.
image-plan:
  - file: Greek_Script_from_Rosetta_Stone_(36140196440).jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Greek script from the Rosetta Stone decree, issued in the Ptolemaic
      Kingdom in the 2nd century BC.
  - file: Diadoch.png
    role: inline
    section: origins-and-history
    caption: The Successor kingdoms before the battle of Ipsus, 303 BC. The new
      common variety was spoken from the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt to the
      Seleucid Empire of Mesopotamia.
  - file: Linguistic_map_of_the_Roman_Empire_c._400.svg
    role: full
    section: origins-and-history
    caption: Linguistic map of the Roman Empire c. 400. In its eastern provinces
      the empire communicated almost exclusively in Greek, even in areas where
      Greek was not the dominant spoken language.
  - file: P46.jpg
    role: inline
    section: types
    caption: Papyrus 46, one of the oldest extant New Testament manuscripts in
      Greek, written on papyrus; its 'most probable date' is between 175 and
      225.
glossary:
  - term: Koine
    gloss: 'Greek for "common": the name comes from ἡ κοινὴ διάλεκτος, "the common
      dialect".'
    from: The English-language name Koine is derived from the Koine Greek term ἡ
      κοινὴ διάλεκτος (hē koinḕ diálektos), meaning "the common dialect".[[ ^5
      ]] The Greek word κοινή (koinḗ) itself means "common".
  - term: Septuagint
    gloss: A 3rd-century BC Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible (Old Testament)
      and texts not included in the Hebrew Bible.
    from: The Septuagint, a 3rd-century BC Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible
      (Old Testament) and texts not included in the Hebrew Bible;
  - term: Patristic Greek
    gloss: A term sometimes used for the Greek written by the Greek Church
      Fathers, the Early Christian theologians in late antiquity.
    from: The term patristic Greek is sometimes used for the Greek written by the
      Greek Church Fathers, the Early Christian theologians in late antiquity.
  - term: Historical present
    gloss: Present tense verbs used in some narrative sections of the New
      Testament to describe events that are in the past with respect to the
      speaker.
    from: The "historical present" tense is a term used for present tense verbs
      that are used in some narrative sections of the New Testament to
      describe events that are in the past with respect to the speaker.
  - term: Isochronic
    gloss: "Having equal length: from the second century BC all Greek vowels were
      pronounced this way, the ancient long/short distinction having been
      lost."
    from: The ancient distinction between long and short vowels was gradually
      lost, and from the second century BC all vowels were isochronic (having
      equal length).
  - term: Psilosis
    gloss: Loss of rough breathing, /h/ — already gone from the Ionic Greek
      varieties of Anatolia and the Aeolic Greek of Lesbos.
    from: "Psilosis: loss of rough breathing, /h/. Rough breathing had already
      been lost in the Ionic Greek varieties of Anatolia and the Aeolic Greek
      of Lesbos."
  - term: Iotacism
    gloss: The changes by which η, and later υ and οι, came to be pronounced as ι.
    from: Simple vowels mostly preserved their ancient pronunciations. η/e/
      (classically pronounced /ɛː/) was raised and merged with ι. In the 10th
      century AD, υ/οι/y/ unrounded to merge with ι. These changes are known
      as iotacism.
  - term: Attic Koine
    gloss: The literary Koine of the Hellenistic age, so called because it
      resembles Attic to such a degree.
    from: The literary Koine of the Hellenistic age resembles Attic in such a
      degree that it is often mentioned as Attic Koine.
further:
  - slug: alexander-the-great
    why: Koine arose as a common dialect within his armies, and the post-Classical
      period of Greek is dated from his death in 323 BC.
  - slug: macedonia-ancient-kingdom
    why: Under the leadership of Macedon the new common variety spread from Egypt
      to Mesopotamia, and Ancient Macedonian contributed considerable elements
      to it.
  - slug: rosetta-stone
    why: The Greek script this page opens on comes from its decree, issued in the
      Ptolemaic Kingdom in the 2nd century BC.
anchors:
  hook: Koine is also the language of the Septuagint (the 3rd century BC Greek
    translation of the Hebrew Bible), the Christian New Testament, and of most
    early Christian theological writing by the Church Fathers. In this
    context, Koine Greek is also known as "Biblical", "New Testament",
    "ecclesiastical", or "patristic" Greek. The Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius
    wrote his private thoughts in Koine Greek in a work that is now known as
    Meditations. Koine Greek continues to be used as the liturgical language
    of services in the Greek Orthodox Church and in some Greek Catholic
    churches.
  standfirst: It evolved from the spread of Greek following the conquests of
    Alexander the Great in the fourth century BC, and served as the lingua
    franca of much of the Mediterranean region and the Middle East during the
    following centuries. It was based mainly on Attic and related Ionic speech
    forms, with various admixtures brought about through dialect levelling
    with other varieties.
